Richard,

Gerhard Smit here, CTTX in Gqeberha. We engineer owned networks on lodges,
reserves and farms; we don't sell packages.

Amakhala runs 11 lodges and camps on one reserve, with an Equine APU, K9
unit and aerial surveillance behind them, yet most of the lodges list
Wi-Fi only in the public areas by the main building. Each camp paying for
its own link and rented Wi-Fi means several bills and still no network
where rangers and field staff work. One owned backbone fixes both: every
camp, gate and camera on the same monitored network, with the carrier link
feeding it at a single point.

I've copied Dwain as reserve manager, since field coverage is where this
matters most. I've already done a desktop terrain study of Amakhala from
satellite elevation data; it's attached. It shows where the backbone
corridors and the uplink could run, with every lodge we could locate on a
clear radio path. The site positions are desktop candidates until we walk
them.

The next step is a half-day assessment on site: what you have, what fails,
what it costs you, and what an owned network would look like. R3,500 ex
VAT, credited against the build, and the report is yours either way. If
you share the lodges' current connectivity invoices, I'll put Amakhala's
actual payback in that report.

Can I call you for 10 minutes this week?

